      "hints": [
        "Apply the chain rule to $(3-2x)^3$ to get $\\frac{dy}{dx} = -6(3-2x)^2 + 24$.",
        "Differentiate again to find $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = 24(3-2x)$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Differentiate $y = (3 - 2x)^3 + 24x$ with respect to $x$ using the chain rule on the first term:\n$$\\frac{dy}{dx} = 3(3 - 2x)^2 \\times (-2) + 24 = -6(3 - 2x)^2 + 24$$",
        "Differentiate again with respect to $x$ to find the second derivative:\n$$\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = -6 \\times 2(3 - 2x) \\times (-2) = 24(3 - 2x)$$",
        "State both derivative expressions:\n$$\\frac{dy}{dx} = -6(3 - 2x)^2 + 24, \\quad \\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = 24(3 - 2x) = 72 - 48x$$"
      ]
    },
    {
      "part_id": "9709_s20_11_q9_b",
      "difficulty": 3,
      "mapping": {
        "topic_id": "9709_p1_t_differentiation",
